# cockpit-secrets .envdefault
#
# deploy.sh seeds this to [install path]/.env, MISSING-ONLY: an existing .env is
# never overwritten, because it is what the operator decided. When a new version
# adds a key, deploy.sh says so and install.sh refuses until you add it.
#
# GRAMMAR (docs/DEPLOY-CONTRACT.md section 4.1) - a strict subset of what sh,
# systemd's EnvironmentFile and Python all accept, so one file feeds all three:
#   KEY=value | KEY="value with spaces" | KEY=
#   Keys match ^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*$. No `export`. Full-line comments only - a
#   trailing # is part of the value. NO $, no ${...}, no $(...), no backticks:
#   there is no interpolation. Write the value out in full, or let the consumer
#   join the two halves.
#
# ===========================================================================
# THIS FILE NEVER CONTAINS A SECRET. Not a passphrase, not a key, not a token.
# ===========================================================================
# It carries LOCATIONS AND SETTINGS. It is mode 0644 root:root on purpose
# (section 4.2, JC-8): 0600 would force the user-class helper - the one that
# runs as the logged-on user, not root - to escalate in order to read a
# directory name, which is a worse trade than an operator learning where the
# payload lives. deploy.sh REFUSES to write a .env whose key looks like a
# credential and whose value is not a path to one, and that refusal is what
# makes 0644 safe rather than merely convenient.
#
# Safe FILES, key files and passphrases live in SECRETS_ETC_DIR/safes at 0700
# root:root. They are named by the registry, never by this file.
#
# A .env in the SOURCE checkout is TEST-ONLY. It exists so the suite and a dev
# install have something to read, it is in .gitignore, and no deployed plugin
# ever reads one - the helper resolves its configuration through
# /etc/cockpit-secrets/install.conf and has no "look beside me" step at all
# (section 4.3).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THESE ARE NOT UNDER THE INSTALL PATH
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every location below is in /etc, /var/lib or /var/log rather than in
# /opt/cockpit-secrets. An upgrade replaces the payload WHOLESALE, so nothing
# an upgrade replaces may hold state - and backup policy, logrotate, SELinux
# labelling and restorecon already know those three trees. They do not know
# /opt/cockpit-secrets/state, and a second state directory where /var/lib would
# have done is a backup that silently does not cover you.
#
# install.sh REFUSES if any value here disagrees with the constant compiled
# into secrets-admin. Two places that can hold a location is one place too
# many, and a split between them would be invisible until a safe went missing.

# The registry root: <this>/safes.d holds one *.json per safe, and that
# directory IS the access-control policy for every safe on this host. The
# helper refuses to trust it at all if it, or any entry in it, is group- or
# other-writable (KNOWN_ISSUES I1). <this>/safes holds admin-class safe files
# at 0700. Both survive an upgrade and an uninstall.
SECRETS_ETC_DIR=/etc/cockpit-secrets

# Runtime state. <this>/state holds the per-(uid, safe) lockout counters (I16) -
# uninstalling must not be a way to clear a lockout, which is why this is
# outside the payload. <this>/exports is the export destination (I21): if
# anybody ever ran the export verb, a file in there is AN ENTIRE SAFE IN
# PLAINTEXT. Look in it before you remove anything, and shred rather than rm.
SECRETS_VAR_DIR=/var/lib/cockpit-secrets

# The audit log: who opened what, and when. It carries no value and no path
# (I15), but it is still a map of who holds what, so it is 0700 and it outlives
# the tool.
SECRETS_LOG_DIR=/var/log/cockpit-secrets

# Where the helper imports backends/ and schema/ from. install.sh links the
# payload's lib/ here per file; secrets-admin looks here after failing to find
# the package beside itself, which is what makes the repo layout and the
# installed layout both work with no code change.
SECRETS_LIB_DIR=/usr/local/lib/cockpit-secrets

# Shipped documentation - the per-user registry example lives here. It is
# documentation, NOT policy: a per-user entry in the system registry would be an
# entry naming a path in somebody's home directory, read by a root helper.
SECRETS_EXAMPLE_DIR=/usr/local/share/cockpit-secrets/examples

# The unit directory (--with-agent only). JC-11: the project picks one and
# records it, so --uninstall on a host installed by a different version still
# finds the units. User units go in <this>/user, the system template in
# <this>/system. Nothing here is ever enabled by an installer.
SECRETS_UNIT_DIR=/usr/local/lib/systemd
